# Copy to backend/.env and fill in at least one LLM provider key below.
# Every LLM call (wizard chat, classification, extraction) tries providers in
# LLM_PROVIDER_ORDER and falls through to the next on failure — set more than
# one to ride out a free-tier rate limit instead of the request just failing.

# Free key: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey (no card needed in most regions)
# The only provider used for image input (English scanned-page fallback) —
# see README's "Known limitation: English OCR".
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
# Optional 2nd/3rd Gemini key from a DIFFERENT account/project — each becomes
# its own "gemini_2"/"gemini_3" entry in the orders below with an
# independent rate-limit cooldown (see config.py's comment), so a 429 on the
# main key falls through to a fresh quota instead of giving up. Blank = skip.
GEMINI_API_KEY_2=
GEMINI_API_KEY_3=

# Free key: https://console.groq.com/keys — fast, generous free tier, text-only here.
GROQ_API_KEY=
GROQ_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.8-27b
# Optional 2nd Groq key (different account) — same rotation idea as Gemini above.
GROQ_API_KEY_2=

# Free key: https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens — least reliable link in the
# chain (hosted model availability varies); mainly a last-resort fallback.
# You may need to change HUGGINGFACE_MODEL to whatever your account currently
# has free router access to.
HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY=
HUGGINGFACE_MODEL=meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct

# Free key: https://cloud.cerebras.ai/ — Cerebras runs models on its own
# inference hardware, which is dramatically faster than a CPU-only local
# Ollama call and not subject to Gemini/Groq's shared free-tier queues.
# Which models your account can actually call (varies per account/plan) —
# check with: `curl https://api.cerebras.ai/v1/models -H "Authorization: Bearer $CEREBRAS_API_KEY"`.
# A 402 Payment Required on every model means the account itself needs
# billing/credits set up before any chat-completions call will succeed —
# that's a Cerebras account issue, not a config problem here.
CEREBRAS_API_KEY=
CEREBRAS_MODEL=gpt-oss-120b

# Free key: https://openrouter.ai/keys — aggregates many hosted models
# (including several free ones) behind one OpenAI-compatible API. Free model
# availability changes often (there was no free Qwen model at all as of this
# writing) — list what's currently free with:
#   curl https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models | python -c "import json,sys; print([m['id'] for m in json.load(sys.stdin)['data'] if ':free' in m['id']])"
# A zero-balance account also gets a low daily free-tier request cap — a 429
# here isn't necessarily a config problem.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_MODEL=google/gemma-4-31b-it:free

# --- Extra free-tier providers (all optional; blank = skipped, same as above) ---
# Free key: https://cloud.sambanova.ai/apis — fast dedicated inference hardware.
SAMBANOVA_API_KEY=
SAMBANOVA_MODEL=Meta-Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct

# Free key: https://console.mistral.ai/api-keys — "La Plateforme" free tier.
MISTRAL_API_KEY=
MISTRAL_MODEL=mistral-small-latest

# Free key: https://build.nvidia.com — NVIDIA's hosted model catalog (NIM).
NVIDIA_API_KEY=
NVIDIA_MODEL=meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct

# GitHub personal access token with the "models: read" permission —
# https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens — a plain GitHub PAT,
# not a provider-specific API key. See https://docs.github.com/en/github-models.
GITHUB_TOKEN=
GITHUB_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# Amazon Nova on AWS Bedrock (llm/providers.py's NovaProvider) — the one
# PAID provider in this file (Bedrock per-token; Nova Lite is among the
# cheapest available), and the only one that is neither free-tier
# rate-limited nor bound by your CPU. No API key line: it authenticates via
# the ambient AWS credential chain, the same one OCR_LAMBDA_* below uses, so
# it needs AWS credentials with bedrock:InvokeModel. Blank NOVA_MODEL =
# provider skipped entirely, same convention as every key above.
#
# NOVA_MODEL must be the REGION-PREFIXED inference-profile ID, not the bare
# model ID — Bedrock rejects on-demand invocation of this model generation by
# bare ID with ValidationException ("Retry your request with the ID or ARN of
# an inference profile that contains this model"):
#   us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0   correct
#   amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0      rejected
# NOVA_REGION is independent of OCR_LAMBDA_REGION — Bedrock model
# availability differs by region, so keep them separate.
# Nova also accepts image input, making it a second vision-capable provider
# alongside Gemini for the scanned-page fallback below.
NOVA_MODEL=
NOVA_REGION=us-east-1
# Not a normal-case limit — headroom. With no cap, Bedrock's own default
# silently truncated a dense document's JSON reply mid-object, which then
# failed to parse at all; a report with hundreds of test rows needs room.
NOVA_MAX_TOKENS=65535

# Ollama-only for every text-based call (wizard chat, classification, per-
# doc-type field extraction) is the default here — Gemini is kept configured
# ONLY because Ollama structurally can't take image input (OllamaProvider
# raises immediately, no network call, for any image= call — see
# llm/providers.py); that instant, harmless failure is what lets the chain
# fall through to Gemini for the one case Ollama can never handle: reading a
# scanned page directly when local OCR (RapidOCR) itself fails. Add
# gemini_2/gemini_3/groq/groq_2/huggingface/sambanova/mistral/nvidia/github
# back into this order (their keys can stay configured below either way) if
# you'd rather have more fallback/rotation depth for text calls too — e.g. to
# ride out Ollama being slow/unavailable, or one Gemini/Groq key's quota
# running out, rather than treat Ollama+one key as the only text providers.
# Every name here safely no-ops if its key is blank (see llm/factory.py), so
# there's no cost to listing one before you've actually configured it.
# Add "nova" (usually first) if you've set NOVA_MODEL above and want a
# provider that won't rate-limit or depend on local CPU — note it bills per
# token, unlike everything else in this order.
# Free-tier model lineups change often —
# if a model 404s, list what's currently available:
# `curl https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/models -H "Authorization: Bearer $GROQ_API_KEY"`
# or `curl "https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models?key=$GEMINI_API_KEY"`.
LLM_PROVIDER_ORDER=ollama,gemini
LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# Local model via Ollama (https://ollama.com/download) — no API key, no rate
# limit, no hard request-size cap. Install, then `ollama pull qwen2.5:3b` (or
# a bigger model if your machine has the RAM/VRAM for it — 3b is sized for a
# modest, CPU-only machine). Nothing else to configure; a missing/unreachable
# Ollama just fails over to the next provider in whichever order includes it.
# If C: is short on space, redirect model storage before pulling:
#   setx OLLAMA_MODELS D:\ollama-models   (then restart the Ollama service)
# If nothing's currently resident in RAM, Ollama cold-loads the model from
# disk before answering at all — measured ~45s for that alone on a modest
# machine, before a real (longer) extraction prompt even starts generating.
# MEASURED 2026-08-30 (12th-gen i5, qwen2.5:3b Q4): a 24-field whole-form
# section took 297s asking the model to spell out a null for every absent
# field — right at the old 300s ceiling. Telling it to omit absent fields
# instead (see extractor.py's extract_section_fields) cut that to 115s warm.
# 210s balances a cold-started, moderately-full section finishing against a
# genuinely stuck call falling back in bounded time; a very dense section can
# still legitimately exceed it and fall back to Gemini/etc — that's the
# intended safety net, not a bug.
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5:3b
OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=210
# 0 = use every logical core (see llm/providers.py's OllamaProvider) rather
# than whatever conservative default Ollama picks on a hybrid CPU.
OLLAMA_NUM_THREAD=0
# A safety ceiling, not a normal-case constraint — comfortably above what
# even a fully-populated 24-field section needs.
OLLAMA_NUM_PREDICT=1024

# documents/pipeline.py's completed_application_form (whole-form) extraction
# uses this order instead of LLM_PROVIDER_ORDER — Ollama-only by default here.
# Never sends images (only text chunks), so there's no vision-fallback
# exception to carve out the way LLM_PROVIDER_ORDER above needs one.
#
# For a short (<=1-3 page) upload this is exactly ONE call for the whole
# document (see pipeline.py's _SINGLE_CALL_MAX_CHARS), so Ollama-only just
# means that one call waits for Ollama specifically rather than racing to
# whichever cloud provider answers first. For a genuinely long (15-30 page)
# form that still goes through the older many-call per-section path (10+ LLM
# calls for one document), keep at least one cloud provider in this list too
# if you want resilience there — with only "ollama" and nothing else, one
# section's Ollama call being slow backs it off for a bit (llm/chain.py's
# cooldown), and with no fallback configured every later section fails
# outright too since there's nothing left to try (this happened for real —
# see config.py's ollama_timeout_seconds comment for the investigation).
# Every extra name here (gemini_2/gemini_3/groq_2/sambanova/mistral/nvidia/
# github/...) safely no-ops while its key is blank.
CHUNKED_EXTRACTION_PROVIDER_ORDER=ollama,gemini,gemini_2,gemini_3,groq,groq_2,cerebras,openrouter,sambanova,mistral,nvidia,github,huggingface

# deepreef-ocr: invoked directly via the AWS SDK (boto3 lambda.invoke), same
# as pcsapaiv2 production. Needs AWS credentials in the environment (profile
# or access keys) with lambda:InvokeFunction on this function.
OCR_LAMBDA_FUNCTION_NAME=akash-ocr
OCR_LAMBDA_REGION=ap-south-1
OCR_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# Local OCR for English/Latin scans is RapidOCR (documents/local_ocr.py) —
# the same engine (rapidocr-onnxruntime) the deepreef-ocr sibling project's
# Lambda runs for every other script, using RapidOCR's own bundled default
# model instead of one of deepreef-ocr's baked non-English .onnx files, in
# process (no AWS call, no Docker). Real per-line bounding boxes the LLM
# vision fallback never could give. No config needed here: `pip install
# rapidocr-onnxruntime` pulls everything required, and its default model
# ships inside the package itself — nothing downloads at runtime.

# RAG pipeline for the "completed_application_form" doc_type (15+ page whole
# form uploads) — see documents/chunking.py, embeddings.py, vector_store.py,
# retrieval.py. Sentence Transformers embeds each page; Qdrant runs embedded
# (on-disk, no server/Docker) so only the pages relevant to a given form
# section get sent to the LLM at all.
EMBEDDING_MODEL=all-MiniLM-L6-v2
QDRANT_STORAGE_DIR=./qdrant_storage
RETRIEVAL_TOP_K=4

DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///./nabl.db
STORAGE_DIR=./storage
CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD=0.85
